A||||13||beer_stud_21||An affordable wood dash kit?||||1||
Z||000000||beer_stud_21||10-28-2001||02:44 AM||||I've got something I would like to run by you people.  "hypothetically speaking" If someone was to offer real wood interior kits made from 1-4 different kinds of beautiful/rare woods would anyone be interested?  Price would depend on the wood used, but would be much cheaper (in price, not quality) than the ones the fiero store sells.  ($299.00-$349.00)  There is also the possibility of custom dash/center console/rally guages/ etc if the demand is there.  Even possibly custom color (dyed) dashes to complement your interior.  All of this is "hypothetical" remember.<P>Do people even <B>like</B> wood interiors?  I havn't seen to many in pictures.  Is that because they are to expensive?  I know I would never buy a fierostore one for $350.00!!<P>Feedback!  I want Feedback!<P>-marlin<p>[This message has been edited by beer_stud_21 (edited 10-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||beer_stud_21||10-28-2001||02:16 PM||||Bumpity, bump, bump!||||reg||1
Z||000002||85ZainoGT||10-28-2001||02:50 PM||||I am hopefully buying a Gold 87gt this weekend, and I was thinking of getting the wood look for the interior, so ya, I would be really interested, maybe my car could be your ghiney pig?  ill promote your stuff for you  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  e-mail me if your interested!<P>------------------<BR>Paul Ventimiglia<BR>1985 White/Silver GT <BR>2.8L 4 Speed LOADED :)<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sigpic2529_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||beer_stud_21||10-28-2001||03:27 PM||||I will be offering a discount to the first few people who have both a good looking interior, and a good quality digital camera that can take pictures for me.  Would anyone be interested in having the radio space either made smaller to fit a normal sized stereo or moved up into the middle of the console?  Anyone else have ideas for me?<P>-Marlin||||reg||1
Z||000004||RossT||10-28-2001||03:36 PM||||Real wood, not just a stick on overylay??<P>Would they "replace" the bezeles, or would you still have to stick or glue them on to your current ones?<P>I am interested too.  <P>I would like the lighter tone woods over the usual rosewood that you see.  I think that the light pine colors would look good in my 33,000 mile yellow formula.<P>I also have a good digital camera!<P>Most of the wood ones I have seen are just stick on wood grain paper.  The real wood ones do look very good.<P>Also the side vents would look good in wood too.<P>Ross<P>[This message has been edited by RossT (edited 10-28-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by RossT (edited 10-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||85ZainoGT||10-28-2001||04:36 PM||||heh! I have a digital camera as you previously mentioned and would be more than happy to promote for you... I dont yet have a cd player, but as long as I can buy a cd player to fit in the wood, im good to go, do whatcha do!  let me know what you need from me if you want me to help!  my e-mail is in my profile!<P>   *~Paul Ventimiglia||||reg||14
Z||000006||Monkeyman||10-28-2001||04:42 PM||||You can't really move even a standard DIN size stereo up much.  If you'll remove the face, you'll see why.  The only way you could move a DIN size stereo up any would be with a custom bracket.  The ones you can buy from either Wally World or a stereo shop will only center it in the opening.||||reg||1
Z||000007||lou||10-28-2001||04:58 PM||||I have a wood interior and the key here is to offer all the pieces not just the major pieces. FOCOA used to sell a kit that was Mika no real wood but it came with 18 pieces including one for the aftermarket glove box. Right now there are no kits available with all the pieces that kit had. I was looking for a full kit for a while and had to settle for what was available.  By the way I am a professional photographer and would be interested in working out a trade with you .  email if you are interested.||||reg||1
Z||000008||beer_stud_21||10-28-2001||05:11 PM||||I am still working out the details and definitely appreciate all your input.  I don't want to put the time in if no one is interested, but that doesn't look to be the case.  I have noticed that the radio cannot be moved up very far, but would it be useful to move it to the top of the hole?(top of the 1.5 din opening)  It would make it a bit easier to get to while driving...  Another question:  In an ideal world what interior parts would you like to see in the "kit"  besides the guage cluster, center console, rally guages, and shifter console?  <P>Thanks for the input everyone.  Let me know if you want to beta test/photograph the final version and I will pull names from a hat or something. (when the time comes of course)<P>-Marlin||||reg||1
Z||000009||beer_stud_21||10-28-2001||05:19 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B>Real wood, not just a stick on overylay??<P>Would they "replace" the bezeles, or would you still have to stick or glue them on to your current ones?<P>I am interested too. <P>I would like the lighter tone woods over the usual rosewood that you see.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>They would be <B>real</B> wood, not stick on.  I think the stick on kind are tacky.  The main wood I have in mind is quite blonde most of the time.  It sometimes has some natural color, but also takes well to dye, so there is the possibility of dying the wood any color you choose. ||||reg||1
Z||000010||RossT||10-28-2001||05:45 PM||||Beer stud, put me on your list! ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Old Lar||10-28-2001||07:22 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Dash.jpg"> <BR> South American Rosewood veneer wood dash kit for my 88.  Looks nice wit the tan interior.  Less than $200. ||||reg||1
Z||000012||maryjane||10-28-2001||09:24 PM||||Beer Stud, You have access to a CNC machining center? Makes things a lot faster once the master program is written. I've not made any for a Fiero, but have made 4 sets for older Dodge trucks. Used red oak and native mesquite, but only machined out the parts, od's, cut-outs, slots and gauge apertures.I let the truck owners do all the drilling for mounting. Used up-shear-down-shear 4 fluted router bits at about 3000 rpm, to prevent little burrs and splinters. <BR>Don't have any pics, and just as favors to my friends during slow times at work. Good luck. Old Lars, yours look great!||||reg||1
Z||000013||MinnGreenGT||10-29-2001||09:43 AM||||I'm always interested in the wood dash upgrade... The best place I've found for the synthetic materials is here: <A HREF="http://www.autodash.com/model.lasso" TARGET=_blank>http://www.autodash.com/model.lasso</A>   Total for base kit w/all options is about $239.<P>Old Lar- I love you veneer finish... what did you use to coat it?  A polyurethane finish or lacquer?  Looks awsome!||||reg||1
